{# Bundle base: the SHELL's page frame plus the sheets the Files hub needs.

   Every screen of this bundle extends this rather than the shell directly, so
   the hub's vocabulary loads only where the hub renders and the preview overlay
   is included exactly once per page.

   THE FRAME IS THE SHELL'S (uhifadhi/shell-module), not this module's. Before
   the fleet had a shell, these pages extended a `layout.html.twig` the
   application was expected to provide, and typed their own `<div class="page">`
   — which is why the page furniture below moved out: the trail, the page head
   and the flashes are the frame's sockets now, and a module that types its own
   copies is a module that drifts from every other screen in the installation.

   THE WIDGET MODULE'S SHEET IS NOT OPTIONAL. The hub IS a widget dashboard, so
   `.w-grid`, `.w-cell`, `.w-span-*` and the whole library chrome are drawn by
   that bundle's stylesheet, which its own header says must be linked by the page
   rendering it. Without it the grid has nothing declaring a span and every
   widget renders one column wide.

   Ordered shell → widget → preview → hub, weakest to strongest: this module's
   own sheets are the ones allowed to override, and they are last.

   Symfony docs — https://symfony.com/doc/current/frontend/asset_mapper.html :
   "All bundles that have a Resources/public/ or public/ directory will
   automatically have that directory added as an asset path, using the namespace:
   bundles/<BundleName>" — and under AssetMapper those assets are automatically
   content-versioned. So public/files.css is served as
   bundles/uhifadhistorage/files.css with a digest, no assets:install. #}
{% extends '@UhifadhiShell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Widget\\UhifadhiWidgetBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
    {# The PREVIEW's vocabulary next: it belongs to the shareable component, not
       to the hub, and another module's page loads exactly this one file without
       files.css. The hub is one of its consumers, so it loads it the same way. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/uhifadhistorage/preview.css') }}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/uhifadhistorage/files.css') }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block javascripts %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {# The hub's OWN behaviour: the filter row, the shape toggle and the removal
       form. Plain DOM — these run on the hub's four screens only and nothing
       outside this bundle asks for them. The preview's behaviour is a Stimulus
       controller instead, because other modules' pages consume it and it has to
       arm itself wherever it is included. #}
    <script src="{{ asset('bundles/uhifadhistorage/files.js') }}" defer></script>
{% endblock %}

{# THE HUB IS ORG-WIDE, so it fills the tab socket with nothing. The shell's
   default there is an area's tab strip, and the Files hub is deliberately about
   every area at once — a strip naming one of them would be the page claiming a
   place it is not in. #}
{% block shell_page_tabs %}{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}
    {% block storage_page %}{% endblock %}
    {# The preview, once per page, for every tile and row drawn above it. #}
    {{ include('@UhifadhiStorage/overlay/_preview.html.twig') }}
{% endblock %}
